    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Graham (1996a) summarizes the differences between object-oriented and traditional testing in two ways. First, she notes which aspects of object orientation make testing easier and which make it harder. For example, objects tend to be small, and the complexity that might ordinarily reside in the component is often pushed instead toward the interfaces among components. This difference means that unit testing is less difficult, but integration testing must be much more extensive. As we have seen, encapsulation is often considered a positive attribute of object-oriented design, but it also requires more extensive integration testing.",
